uldn't still be  talking about this as a headline at this  point but uh the the issues of of how do  you have a hybrid parliament and how  much are parties going to work together  and how much will politics play into  that we're still dealing with that  pandemic we're still having these you  know weekly or sometimes more updates  from public health this is still top of  mind it's still affecting so many  people's lives that for me it stood out  and also this sense of of things feeling  the same i mean  david touched on the the idea of the  election turning out the same result but  we're we're in essentially the same  place in terms of the the breakdown in  the house from parties and it feels very  much like we're in the same place uh in  terms of of covet and and having these  same conversations about restrictions  and and the state of things so this idea  of sameness i think stands out for me  across 2021 both on the politics and on  the public health front yeah it really  does feel um a little bit like the song  that never ends or groundhog day  whatever your cultural comparison is um  that a lot of us are still processing  march 2020 i think i feel like i'm still  in april of 2020 but at the end of 2021  mike what stood out for you yeah i think  you know to everybody's point yeah covet  19 the election i'm gonna go off the  board for a second here and i think the  highlight for me was unanimous consent  on conversion therapy that was something  that i think nobody expected in this  parliament we came back to parliament  with the same result as 2019 and a lot  of people looked at and went well now  we're going to have the same political  games and then somehow the day that it  came on the floor of the house of  commons the conservatives surprised  everybody and call for a  vote on unanimous consent to pass  conversion therapy why i think this is  important not only because i covered the  story but the ramifications of this one  that the conservatives were trying to  put this issue behind them so that it  wouldn't continue to hang over them to  speaking to survivors of it and doing  the story so touching to hear from these  people who said that this is so  important to them to finally get through  this to finally have this being illegal  on the books and not only that it  happened in the house of commons  unanimous consent also in the senate  that how quickly this was passed maybe  gives me a little bit of hope going into  the new year that potentially this  parliament can work towards a better end  for canadians and you know talking to  that survivor exchanging emails with him  after it was passed how shocked he was  but so happy he was that this is finally  passed through the house certainly a big  relief to a lot of people and a rare  showing of not just bipartisan but all  party support for this for something  that people really felt needed to happen  and was the right thing it's always nice  in politics when they're not squabbling  and they come together on important  issues david do you think we're going to  see that trend continue or is it going  to devolve into more partisan bickering  as we head into 2022  there was a different kind of partisan  bickering and it's it's from those who  are very angry at the current public  health environment and again this goes  back to the election all of us were out  on the road with various leaders we  traveled around the country and uh you  know uh abigail i think was there when  people were throwing rocks at the prime  minister some of which hit members of  the media we were all there to watch  angry mobs  using the most foul-mouthed obscenities  often with kids in their arms yelling at  political candidates didn't matter the  candidate it was new democrats it was  liberals  i know conservative premiers were  experiencing this same sort of threats  of violence i've talked to mps of all  parties who say they've never seen an  electorate so angry anger turning into  threats threats sometimes turning into  violence we're going to have an election  in ontario next year in 2022 and i know  that doug ford's conservatives they're  the incumbent uh and they've been you  know right there in lockstep with every  other province imposing lockdowns and  public health measures to keep us safe  and they are expecting to see the same  kind of angry mobs at their rallies that  justin trudeau's liberals saw at theirs  so that was a low light for 2021 and  that's something to keep an eye on for  2022.  abigail you were there you experienced  it do you think that this is going to  continue into the next year  you know i i don't want to make any  predictions there because it was just so  uh incomprehensible to watch in turn  just in terms not not incompetent  comprehensible that people are  frustrated not incomprehensible that  people are fed up with what's going on  but you know david touched on it to see  this level of anger is something i've  never witnessed before to to feel that  tension in those crowds we all we all  knew that at some point this was going  to escalate you talk about the the rock  or the gravel or however you want to  describe what was thrown through the the  crowds that that evening but we all  could sense that that there was going to  be an escalation there just because of  the level of vitriol and and the the  constant profanity and like you say you  know people holding babies and just  screaming their heads off with such a  level of anger so i you know i hope that  that we don't see that again going  forward but i certainly uh you know was  was caught off guard by the the level of  intensity and and how long it lasted for  that i wouldn't want to make a  prediction going forward mike finally  yeah i'm a little concerned that this is  a line that we've crossed now and that  people think that this is going to be  permissible going forward and that uh  looking at how it all played out i mean  abigail you were there everybody who you  know was on that liberal campaign  felt that vitriol from people and and  you know understanding that people now  feel like they have the ability to do  this and they're allowed to do this and  i hope that we can all take away from  this campaign that we look at and go no  that was the line that we will never  cross again unfortunately i don't have a  lot of faith in that because i think  that people think  you know i did it once i can do this  again because i have a voice i'm allowed  to do this and i'm allowed to do this as  you said in front of kids and i'm really  worried that going ahead in  future elections that people are going  to say hey i'm going to do this again  yeah i think that we're all concerned  and we all hope for a better 2022 in  many ways let's hope that when we're  back here a year from now it's not  another covid19 discussion maybe our  politics are a little bit more positive  thank you to the three of you for  joining us and for all your hard work  this year  colleen back to you mercedes stevenson  thank you  next why universities in hong kong are  removing monuments that mark the  tiananmen massacre  another sign things are changing in hong  kong three universities this week  removed works of art public monuments  commemorating the 1989 tiananmen  massacre they're the latest examples of  the chinese government's crackdown on  political dissent and a further erosion  of the one country two systems principle  that was supposed to apply to hong kong  mike armstrong explains  it is part of an effort some have said  to erase public memory early thursday  morning crews worked behind barriers  watched by guards dismantling a monument  the danish sculptor who created the work  says he was shocked in the middle of the  night 12 o'clock in hong kong just  one day before christmas evening  i think it's a really brutal way to do  this kind of thing it really really puts  that way  video that was shot it appears the  monument was cut apart packed up in  pieces and then put in a container the  spot where it stood for 24 years is now  empty  this man says the government is trying  to wipe out history people he says have  to fight against forgetting  it was called the pillar of shame an  eight meter tall sculpture depicting  twisted and pained bodies representing  the victims of the tiananmen massacre  in 1989 chinese troops broke up  demonstrations in beijing calling for  greater political freedoms the  government would say 200 civilians were  killed others have said the number was  closer to ten thousand  for decades there were vigils in hong  kong to mark the anniversary two years  ago they were banned as they are in  mainland china instead students  protested by cleaning the artwork other  vigils were larger thousands marched  despite the ban but dozens of activists  and politicians were arrested and  prosecuted  now two other hong kong universities  have also removed artwork inspired by  the tiananmen crackdown a painting at  one university was painted over and  another two monuments were pulled down a  statue that had been in this park and a  monument that had been on this wall the  artist responsible for both says they  were removed at night as though by  thieves  he says the universities and the  government are trying to get rid of  parts of history it's made him feel he  says as though the rule of law and  freedom in hong kong are  mike armstrong global news  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm colleen christie we  leave you tonight with images from the  remarkable life of archbishop desmond  tutu the nobel peace prize winner and  racial justice activist died today in  cape town south africa he was 90 years  old  thanks for watching hope you'll join us  here again tomorrow  good night  you
 

